## Claim
Text: "Wearing face masks can cause infections from bacteria such as staphylococcus."
Claim author: Jane Taylor
Claim date: October 24, 2020
Claim origin: https://archive.ph/IOnJa

## Initial Q&A
### What specific scientific studies or peer-reviewed evidence demonstrate that wearing face masks directly causes infections from bacteria such as staphylococcus?
Answer: The search result from the Pasteur Institute provides general information about staphylococcus infections, risk factors (e.g., broken skin, weakened immune systems, medical procedures), and carriage rates, but it does not mention or cite any scientific studies or peer-reviewed evidence demonstrating that wearing face masks directly causes infections from staphylococcus or other bacteria. Therefore, the search result does not contain sufficient information to answer the question.

Source URL: https://www.pasteur.fr/en/medical-center/disease-sheets/staphylococci

### Does the claim refer to all types of face masks (e.g., surgical masks, cloth masks, N95 respirators) or only a specific kind, and does the risk of bacterial infection vary by mask type?
Answer: The search result focuses exclusively on surgical masks, demonstrating that they can become a source of bacterial contamination (including potentially staphylococcus) with extended wear, but it does not address cloth masks or N95 respirators. Therefore, the claim as stated (“wearing face masks”) is overly broad, and the provided evidence only supports a risk for surgical masks, suggesting that the risk of bacterial infection does vary by mask type, though no direct comparison is made in this study.

Source URL: https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30035033/

### Under what conditions of mask use (e.g., duration of wear, frequency of changing, hygiene practices) does the claimed infection risk occur?
Answer: Based on the source, the risk of a staph infection from wearing a face mask is very rare and only possible if the wearer has an open lesion or untreated wound on their face; the source does not specify exact conditions such as duration of wear or frequency of changing masks, but recommends regular mask cleaning, rigorous hand washing, and using moisturizer to prevent skin irritation that could lead to an open wound.

Source URL: https://health-desk.org/articles/is-there-any-evidence-for-a-relationship-between-staph-infections-and-using-masks-to-prevent-the-spread-of-covid-19

### What is the mechanism by which wearing a face mask would cause a staphylococcal infection, such as through moisture buildup, skin irritation, or contamination?
Answer: According to the source, the mechanism by which wearing a face mask could cause a staphylococcal infection involves the mask irritating and chafing the skin, potentially creating a cut or wound; staph infections can then occur only if the bacteria enter through such a skin opening. The source notes that "in order to develop a staph infection, the person wearing the mask would have to have an open lesion or untreated wound on their face," and that while this concern is valid, it is "very rare" for such an infection to actually result from mask use.

Source URL: https://health-desk.org/articles/is-there-any-evidence-for-a-relationship-between-staph-infections-and-using-masks-to-prevent-the-spread-of-covid-19

### Is the claim based on anecdotal reports or clinical case studies, and if so, how representative are those cases of the general population?
Answer: The claim is based on anecdotal reports—specifically, screenshots of individual tweets sharing personal stories of staph infections attributed to mask-wearing. However, multiple dermatologists interviewed for the fact-check stated they have seen no clinical evidence linking masks to such infections and have observed no uptick in staph cases among patients. These anecdotal reports are therefore not representative of the general population; as one doctor noted, "hundreds of thousands of people in health care" have worn masks for decades without these complications.

Source URL: https://www.usatoday.com/story/news/factcheck/2020/09/18/fact-check-staph-infections-common-not-caused-face-masks/5692064002/

### Does the claim account for the possibility that improper mask handling (e.g., touching the mask, reusing disposable masks) rather than mask-wearing itself is the cause of any observed infections?
Answer: The search result does not address whether the claim accounts for improper mask handling (e.g., touching the mask, reusing disposable masks) as a potential cause of infections. Instead, it provides evidence that N95 respirators and medical masks are protective against bacterial colonization and co-infection, directly contradicting the claim that mask-wearing itself causes infections. No information is given about the role of improper mask use.

Source URL: https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24472436/

### Have health authorities such as the World Health Organization or the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention issued statements or guidelines regarding the risk of bacterial infections from wearing face masks?
Answer: The search result does not contain any statements or guidelines from health authorities such as the World Health Organization or the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention regarding the risk of bacterial infections from wearing face masks. It only provides advice from a dermatologist and a biotechnology expert, not from official health organizations.

Source URL: https://clicks.co.za/beauty-blog/article-view/Is-your-mask-a-breeding-ground-for-bacteria-we-chat-to-an-expert

## Final Judgement
**Step 1: Summarize key insights from the fact-check.**  
The evidence shows that while wearing face masks, particularly surgical masks, can become contaminated with bacteria over extended use, the direct causation of staphylococcus infections from mask-wearing is extremely rare and conditional. Specifically, infections would require an open wound or lesion on the face for bacteria to enter, and such cases are not commonly observed in clinical practice. A study on N95 respirators and medical masks actually found them protective against bacterial colonization. Anecdotal reports of infections exist, but dermatologists report no clinical evidence of widespread mask-related staph infections, and hundreds of thousands of healthcare workers have worn masks for years without such complications.

**Step 2: Identify lacking information.**  
All key aspects of the claim have been addressed by the evidence: the mechanism (skin irritation leading to infection via open wounds), the rarity of actual cases, the absence of clinical evidence for widespread infections, and the protective role of masks against bacteria. No further information is needed to make a clear decision.

**Step 3: Conclusion.**  
The claim that wearing face masks can cause infections from bacteria such as staphylococcus is misleading because the evidence demonstrates that such infections are very rare, require pre-existing skin openings, and are not supported by clinical observations or broader health data. The claim ignores the crucial context that masks do not inherently cause infections under normal use, and that healthcare workers who wear masks extensively do not suffer from such infections at notable rates. Therefore, the claim fits the definition of cherry-picking: it is technically possible under very specific conditions but omits important context that would change the impression.  
`cherry-picking`

### Verdict: CHERRY_PICKING

### Justification
The claim is misleading because evidence shows that mask-related staphylococcus infections are extremely rare and conditional, requiring an open wound or lesion on the face for bacteria to enter, and dermatologists report no clinical evidence of widespread cases despite decades of mask use by healthcare workers [health-desk.org](https://health-desk.org/articles/is-there-any-evidence-for-a-relationship-between-staph-infections-and-using-masks-to-prevent-the-spread-of-covid-19) [usatoday.com](https://www.usatoday.com/story/news/factcheck/2020/09/18/fact-check-staph-infections-common-not-caused-face-masks/5692064002/). While surgical masks can become contaminated with extended wear [pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30035033](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30035033/), studies on N95 respirators and medical masks actually show they protect against bacterial colonization [pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24472436](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24472436/). The claim omits this crucial context, making it a case of cherry-picking.